<ArticleLayout slug="what-a-48-hour-binding-proposal-contains" ctaLabel="Get a binding proposal" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">A proposal binds, if it binds at all, to the scope it names. So the scope has to be on the page in units, with the exclusions, the revision rounds, the rights and the rules for change written beside the price.</p>

  <h2>What should a creative production proposal include?</h2>

  <p>Deliverables written as countable units, what the price includes and excludes, revision rounds, rights and usage, timeline and dependencies, acceptance, change control, payment terms, validity, and whether the price is fixed or an estimate. hubStudio publishes a binding proposal within 48 hours of a brief as studio policy. Hold it, and every other proposal, to the same list.</p>

  <p>This page describes production and procurement practice, not legal advice. Its definitions come from US federal procurement rules and from the US commercial production trade body's own bidding documents. Other jurisdictions were not checked for it.</p>

  <p>Start with the document type, because it decides how far the final invoice can move.</p>

