          <td>Whatever the retainer contract defines as the period's scope</td>
          <td>Fixed per period; scope inside it is what moves</td>
          <td>Continuous work across markets and channels</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>No primary definition of a retainer was read for this page. The row describes the model, and the contract has to define the rest.</p>

  <h2>Is it a quote, an estimate or a binding proposal?</h2>

  <p>The words get used loosely. The one body of rules that defines them precisely is US federal purchasing, and its definitions are useful even to a buyer who will never sign a government contract.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In US federal purchasing, a quotation is not an offer and cannot be accepted to form a binding contract. The contract forms when the supplier accepts the buyer's order.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US Federal Acquisition Regulation, section 13.004, effective 13 March 2026, regulation text read at the official host; it governs federal procurement, not private contracts.</p>

  <p>An offer, in the same regulation, is the thing that binds: a response that, if accepted, commits the supplier to perform. The pricing basis then decides what that commitment is worth.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A firm-fixed-price contract carries a price that is not adjusted for what the work actually cost the contractor, which puts the cost risk on the contractor.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US Federal Acquisition Regulation, section 16.202-1, effective 13 March 2026, read at the official host.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Cost-reimbursement contracts pay allowable costs and set an estimate of total cost, which works as a ceiling the contractor exceeds at its own risk unless the buyer approves.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US Federal Acquisition Regulation, section 16.301-1, effective 13 March 2026, read at the official host.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A time-and-materials contract pays fixed hourly rates plus actual materials, provides no positive profit incentive for efficiency, and may be used only when the extent or duration of the work cannot be estimated with any reasonable confidence.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US Federal Acquisition Regulation, section 16.601, effective 13 March 2026, read at the official host.</p>

  <p>Commercial production asks the same question in its own vocabulary, and asks it first.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The 2026 bidding guide asks, before anything is priced, whether the project is firm bid, cost-plus, or a blend.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's bidding reference guide, 2026 edition, downloaded and read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p>If a proposal does not answer that question on its face, ask it in writing before comparing totals. Two totals on two different bases are not comparable.</p>

  <h2>What does "binding" actually commit a supplier to?</h2>

  <p>Less than most buyers assume, and it depends on who is using the word. There is no single legal meaning to rely on, and the page does not offer one.</p>

  <p>The commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines are the clearest published text on a firm bid, and they do not describe a cap.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under those guidelines an accepted firm bid becomes the minimum contract price. The final price adds hours actually used, never less than the time booked, plus any approved overages.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines, January 2026, read in full September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On a cost-plus job under the same guidelines, the bid is submitted the same way, but the final price carries the actual outside costs incurred or committed instead of the estimate.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines, January 2026, read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p>Set that beside the federal firm-fixed-price contract, which is not adjusted for the supplier's cost experience at all. "Firm bid" and "firm fixed price" are two different instruments. A buyer reading "firm" as "capped" is reading the wrong document.</p>

  <p>The one US federal rule read that defines a binding estimate comes from another industry entirely. It is useful here only as an illustration of how a regulator drew the line.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The one US federal regulation read that defines a binding estimate, written for interstate household movers, requires it to say on its face that it binds both parties and covers only the services it names. Extra services reopen it: the mover reaffirms it, writes a new one the customer signs, or both agree in writing to treat it as non-binding.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 49 CFR 375.403, current eCFR text read 10 September 2026; a different industry, cited only to show what "binding" means where a regulator has defined it.</p>

  <p>Read together, the primary texts agree on something narrower than "the price cannot change." A price document binds, if at all, only to the scope it identifies. A change of scope reopens the price through a written change.</p>

  <p>That is why the scope sections matter more than the word on the cover. hubStudio's own pages say its proposal is binding, is revised before signing if the scope changes, and holds for 30 days. They define nothing further, so the rest has to be asked for in writing, from any supplier.</p>

