  <p>The sections below take the ones that carry the most money.</p>

  <h2>How should deliverables be written?</h2>

  <p>As results, in units someone can count and accept. The federal rule on service contracts says it well.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Federal service contracts are told to describe the work in terms of the required results, not how it will be done or how many hours it will take, and to measure performance against stated standards.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US Federal Acquisition Regulation, section 37.602, effective 13 March 2026, read at the official host.</p>

  <p>The trade body's bidding guide pushes the same point into the detail that decides a production budget.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The guide also asks whether the deliverables match the creative that has to be filmed, giving the example of a 15-second deliverable written as a 60 or a 90, and whether every deliverable shares one aspect ratio or some run vertical for social.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's bidding reference guide, 2026 edition, read in full September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The trade body's post-production guidelines call the bid an estimate based on the client's specifications, and say the final deliverables, including versions and aspect ratios, belong in those specifications.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines, January 2026, read in full September 2026; informational guidance, not association policy.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same trade body's 2026 bidding guide tells bidders to establish whether a stated budget is all in, and whether stills, social elements and behind the scenes carry separate decks and separate budgets.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's published bidding reference guide, 2026 edition.</p>

  <p>Versions and aspect ratios multiply fast once a campaign meets a marketplace. The <a href="/solutions/platforms/tmall">Tmall platform page</a> describes the format range one storefront's buying systems ask for, and the <a href="/services/design/video-production">video production service page</a> lists platform reformatting and multi-language adaptation as post-production work. Each of those is a unit, and each belongs in the specification. The <a href="/resources/insights/what-a-finished-brand-asset-costs">what a finished brand asset costs article</a> sets out how to define one asset before pricing it.</p>

  <h2>Who owns the work once it is paid for?</h2>

  <p>Whoever the written agreement says, in both jurisdictions read for this page. Neither default hands the buyer ownership just for paying.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In the United States, a commissioned work counts as a work made for hire only if it falls in one of nine listed categories, part of an audiovisual work among them, and both parties sign a written agreement saying so. Miss any condition and it is not a work made for hire.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US Copyright Office, Circular 30, Works Made for Hire, revised August 2024, read in full September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In China, a commissioned work belongs to whoever the contract names, and to the commissioned party where the contract is silent or absent; transferring economic rights needs a written contract.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: China's Copyright Law as amended 11 November 2020, Articles 19 and 27, statute text read September 2026.</p>

  <p>Usage is a separate question from ownership, and talent makes it expensive.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under the US performers' union commercials agreement effective 1 April 2025, alternate scenes shot for one commercial in the same session are not treated as new commercials, while an edit or lift outside the defined parameters creates a new commercial and new fees.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the union commercials agreement effective April 2025, as read by two independent law firms. The union's own rate documents are not machine readable.</p>

  <p>A proposal should state its usage assumption in one sentence. hubStudio's published policy is one example of what that sentence looks like.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Every hubStudio quote assumes unlimited commercial use across the client's owned and paid channels; broadcast TV, out-of-home at scale and talent-likeness licensing for AI brand ambassadors are quoted separately.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's pricing page, read 10 September 2026, published studio policy.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Ownership of final deliverables passes to the client once hubStudio has received full payment, unless the engagement agreement states otherwise; the proposal or statement of work prevails over the site terms where they conflict.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's terms page, read 10 September 2026, published studio policy.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/resources/copyright-and-ai">copyright and AI resource</a> covers what changes when generated material is part of the work, and the <a href="/resources/insights/ai-brand-ambassadors-what-you-sign">AI brand ambassadors article</a> covers the likeness consent a talent line needs. Both describe production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h2>What happens when the scope changes?</h2>

  <p>The price reopens, in writing, before the extra work starts. The trade body's guidelines set out the mechanism.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">An overage arises when the specifications change, in scope or in schedule, and is billed as a change order. The client names a representative with authority to approve it, and it should be agreed before the work it covers begins.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines, January 2026, read in full September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">If the client does not approve the cost of a specification change, the supplier is not obligated to provide the extra services or deliverables.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines, January 2026, read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p>That second rule cuts both ways. A buyer cannot get unpriced extras by asking loudly, and a supplier cannot bill extras nobody approved. The proposal should name the approver on the buyer's side before production starts.</p>

  <p>Scope can also move before anything is signed. A binding proposal should say what happens then.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">If the scope changes later, hubStudio revises the proposal before anything is signed.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's pricing page, read 10 September 2026, published studio policy.</p>

  <h2>How should payment, validity and cancellation be written?</h2>

  <p>Early, and before any work begins. The trade body's best practices put payment in the bidding phase, not after award.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The trade body's bidding best practices say suppliers should not be expected to begin work until a contract or purchase order commits the buyer to payment, and that payment requirements should be exchanged during the bidding phase.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's suggested best practices for bidding, 2026, read in full September 2026; not association policy or legal advice.</p>

  <p>The same document asks for the award to be confirmed in writing. That confirmation is the acceptance step most proposals leave out.</p>

  <p>On the split itself, there is no norm to copy. The one printed example comes with its own warning.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The post-production guidelines print a payment plan of 75 percent on award and 25 percent on delivery, and state in the same document that the split is an example, not a recommendation.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines, January 2026, read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p>Published studio terms show the patterns a buyer will meet: deposits on unit orders, milestones on projects, monthly billing on retainers.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio takes a deposit upfront on asset orders with the balance on delivery, bills projects against milestones, invoices retainers monthly in advance, and offers Net 30 to established clients and listed companies on request.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's pricing page, read 10 September 2026, published studio policy.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio quotes are valid for 30 days and exclude tax where applicable.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's pricing page, read 10 September 2026, published studio policy.</p>

  <p>Cancellation is the clause buyers skip until they need it. The only published schedule found covers post-production.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same guidelines scale cancellation charges by notice: 100 percent of the awarded bid inside five business days of the start date, 50 percent at six to ten days, 25 percent beyond ten, with committed outside costs and approved overages paid in every case.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines, January 2026, read in full September 2026; guidance for post-production, not a rule and not association policy.</p>

  <p>Treat it as a shape to negotiate, not a standard to accept.</p>

  <h2>What is usually excluded from a production quote?</h2>

  <p>More than the total suggests. Start with the line that is missing from the industry's own form.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US commercial production trade body's standard bid form itemizes a production across sections A through P, from prep crew to breakout talent, and carries no line at all for media usage payments.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's own free bid form, updated January 2023, downloaded and read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p>The trade body's own documents then move three more things outside the price.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The trade body's model language treats placeholder music, sound effects and other offline elements as illustrative only, to be licensed by the agency or advertiser before any use in the finished work.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's language for uncleared materials, November 2025, read in full September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The trade body's post-production guidelines treat working files, project files, 3D data and the supplier's tools and methods as the supplier's own intellectual property, not included in the price of the project.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines, January 2026, read September 2026; the stated position of a suppliers' trade body, not a neutral standard.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The trade body's sample bid language puts tariffs outside the production company's price, and asks the agency to approve an overage for tariff increases incurred after award.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's sample language for bids, dated May 2025, read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p>Use this as an exclusions checklist. Every line should appear in the proposal as either included or excluded, by name.</p>

  <p>- Media usage and talent payments, which the standard bid form has no line for. - Stills, social and behind the scenes, where they are bid as separate decks. - Versions, lengths and aspect ratios missing from the specification. - Stock footage and licensed materials, unless the proposal says they are in. - Placeholder music, sound effects and other uncleared materials. - Working files, project files, 3D data and the supplier's tools. - Tariffs, duties and tax. - Sample logistics, reshoots and approval rounds. - New edits that create new talent fees. - Broadcast TV, out-of-home at scale and talent likeness, where the quote assumes owned and paid channels only.</p>

  <p>If a working file or a trained model matters to you, say so in the brief. A suppliers' trade body has published its position that working files are not part of the price, so silence will not get them for you.</p>

  <h2>How many revision rounds should a proposal include?</h2>

  <p>There is no published number to benchmark against.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No published benchmark with a stated method was found for how many revision rounds creative production includes as standard.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: searches across trade body, analyst and vendor sources logged September 2026; proposal guides ranking for the query advise stating a round count without citing one.</p>

  <p>No published definition of a round turned up either. So the proposal has to supply three things: the count, what counts as one round, and the point where a revision stops being a revision and becomes a change order under the overage rule above.</p>

  <p>hubStudio publishes its own count. It is policy, not a norm.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio includes three rounds of modification on finalized work, more on retainer, and redoes work that misses the brief at no extra charge.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's pricing page, read 10 September 2026, published studio policy.</p>

  <p>Whatever the count, compare it with what triggers the count. Rounds counted on finalized work and rounds counted from the first sketch are different offers.</p>

  <h2>How fast should a production proposal come back?</h2>

  <p>No benchmark exists for this either, and the trade body puts the date in the buyer's hands.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No published benchmark was found for how fast a production proposal should come back. The commercial production trade body's bidding guide treats the bid due date as something the buyer states, and lists a bid forced into a compressed window, such as a weekend, as grounds for a pitch fee.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's bidding reference guide and suggested best practices, 2026 editions, read September 2026, plus a review of 31 search results on four buyer phrasings.</p>

  <p>Speed matters when the number has to go into a budget cycle. It matters less than whether the fast answer is binding.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio sends a binding proposal within 48 hours of receiving a brief, and the quote reflects scope, complexity, volume and the deadline.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's pricing page, read 10 September 2026, published studio policy quoted from the page, not a benchmark.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> sets out how that proposal is scoped. For a formal review with several bidders, <a href="/resources/insights/procurement-guide-buying-ai-content-production">procurement's guide to buying AI content production</a> covers the request for proposal and the scoring. This page stays with the proposal that comes back.</p>

  <h2>What price bands should a proposal land inside?</h2>

  <p>Published category bands, attributed to the category and the date. They tell you whether a proposal is in range. They cannot price your brief, and no hubStudio figure appears among them.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published studio rate cards put a standard product image at $39 to $50, a model or lifestyle image at about $95, a short video clip at about $93 and a single UGC video at about $89, with editing add-ons around $49 each and a finished pro edit at about $199.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, collected September 2026, two independent cards read directly, prices quoted as each card states them.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published rate cards in this category put the floor for ecommerce image editing between roughly $0.25 and $1.20 an image, with one card adding a fixed platform fee of about $95 a month on top.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, collected September 2026, two independent cards read directly and compared, no vendor named.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published unlimited-request design subscription tiers run from roughly $700 to roughly $4,500 a month, month to month, with no capture, no talent and no usage included.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published subscription rate cards in this category, September 2026, two independent pricing pages read directly.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published managed design subscriptions state monthly floors between roughly $6,000 and $15,000, and the one card offering a named dedicated team starts at roughly $30,000 a month on a twelve-month term, plus a $1,000 monthly software fee.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published subscription rate cards in this category, September 2026, published pricing pages only, no vendor named. The $30,000 dedicated floor appears on a single card.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Eight published price surfaces read across two markets price none of sample logistics, reshoots or approval rounds.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, two markets, read directly September 2026, each card read in full for those lines.</p>

  <p>The bands price different units, so they only compare once a proposal is broken down the same way.</p>

