    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The <a href="/resources/production-cost">production cost estimator</a> returns a range built from published rate cards for a scoped brief. It is not a quote, and it contains no hubStudio rate. A range tells you whether a proposal is sane. The proposal tells you what the work costs.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about production proposals</h2>

  <h3>What should a creative production proposal include?</h3>

  <p>Deliverables as countable units, what the price includes and excludes, revision rounds and what counts as one, rights and usage, timeline and client dependencies, acceptance, change control, payment terms, validity and cancellation. It should also say whether the price is a firm bid, a cost-plus estimate or a blend, because that decides how far the final invoice can move.</p>

  <h3>What is the difference between a quote and an estimate?</h3>

  <p>In US federal purchasing, a quotation is not an offer and cannot be accepted to form a contract. An estimate forecasts a cost that moves with actual costs, up to any agreed ceiling. A firm fixed price does not move with the supplier's costs. Usage varies by instrument and by country, so read what the document commits to, not what it is called.</p>

  <h3>What does a binding proposal commit the studio to?</h3>

  <p>The price for the scope it names, and a written change when that scope moves. Under the US commercial production trade body's post-production guidelines, an accepted firm bid is a minimum contract price, with approved overages on top. hubStudio publishes that its proposal is binding, is revised before signing if scope changes, and holds for 30 days. This is production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h3>How many revision rounds should a proposal include?</h3>

  <p>No published benchmark with a stated method sets a number, and no published definition of a round was found. Write the count into the proposal, define what counts as one round, and say where a revision ends and a change order begins. hubStudio publishes three rounds of modification on finalized work, more on retainer, as studio policy.</p>

  <h3>What is usually excluded from a production quote?</h3>

  <p>Media usage and talent payments, which the standard US bid form has no line for. Also placeholder music and uncleared materials, working and project files, tariffs, duties and tax, and any version or aspect ratio missing from the specification. Eight published price surfaces read in September 2026 priced none of sample logistics, reshoots or approval rounds.</p>

  <h3>How fast should a production proposal come back?</h3>

  <p>No published benchmark exists. The US commercial production trade body's bidding guide treats the due date as the buyer's to set, and lists a bid forced into a compressed window, such as a weekend, as grounds for a pitch fee. hubStudio publishes a binding proposal within 48 hours of a brief as studio policy, not as a norm.</p>
